  [BUG] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:150-169  imsic_process_hwirqs
      This is the functional root cause. csr_swap(CSR_MTOPEI, 0) at line 154 reads the full xtopei value containing APLIC-originated PRIO in bits [0:10] and IID in bits [16:27]. Line 155 immediately shifts away the entire PRIO field: 'mirq = (mirq >> IMSIC_TOPEI_ID_SHIFT)'. The switch at lines 157-165 dispatches purely by IID, with IPI hard-coded first and all other interrupts falling to 'unhandled'. The IMSIC_TOPEI_PRIO_MASK (0x7ff) constant is defined at line 36 but never used in any masking or comparison operation. The APLIC-assigned priority that flows through xtopei is consumed by the csr_swap (which auto-claims the interrupt at the IMSIC level) but immediately discarded — no arbitration decision incorporates it. This directly violates the RISC-V AIA spec requirement that software use the priority information encoded in xtopei to make priority-aware dispatch decisions.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:256-286  sbi_trap_aia_irq
      Reads CSR_MTOPI at line 261 and shifts away TOPI_IPRIO_MASK (bits [0:7]) at line 262 with 'mtopi = mtopi >> TOPI_IID_SHIFT'. The while-loop dispatch order at lines 263-282 is fixed by IID: timer → soft → ext → PMU. A high-APLIC-priority external interrupt is always processed after M-mode timer and software interrupts regardless of relative priority. This is priority-blind arbitration at the hart trap level, parallel to the IMSIC-level defect. The RISC-V AIA spec encodes priority in MTOPI bits [0:7] precisely to allow software to compare interrupt priorities across IID classes, but this code discards that field before any comparison can occur.
  [BUG] lib/utils/irqchip/aplic.c:122-192  aplic_init
