PR #694  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: APLIC domain priority/identity information must feed into xtopi major/minor interrupt arbitration; omitting it causes priority inversion where low-priority interrupts starve high-priority ones.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:209-220  aplic_irq_dispatch
      Function accepts prio extracted from APLIC_IDC_CLAIMI bits[7:0] as a parameter but never uses it — only irq and trapframe are forwarded to intr_isrc_dispatch. The hardware-provided priority data is silently discarded at the dispatch boundary, making it impossible for any downstream handler to perform priority-aware scheduling.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:222-246  aplic_intr
      ISR loop unconditionally drains ALL pending interrupts via CLAIMI in hardware-returned order: while ((claimi = aplic_read(...)) != 0). No software-level priority comparison, no preemption check, no re-evaluation of highest-priority pending interrupt between iterations. In an AIA system, xtopi/xtopei would select the single highest-priority interrupt across all pending sources; here the loop services all pending interrupts sequentially, allowing a low-priority interrupt to delay a high-priority one.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:503-537  aplic_bind_intr
      Writes APLIC_TARGET with APLIC_MK_IRQ_TARGET(sc, cpu, APLIC_INTR_DEF_PRIO) where APLIC_INTR_DEF_PRIO is a compile-time constant 1 for every interrupt. This flat-priority configuration writes identical priority values into every hart index target register. In an AIA system, the target priority field feeds into xtopei/xtopi arbitration — identical priorities render priority-based arbitration meaningless even if xtopi hardware were present and operational.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:207-239  aplic_handle_idc_claimi
      Linear scan (for i=0; i<nirqs; i++) finds the first index-ordered pending IRQ matching the target hart, not the highest-priority one. Priority is hardcoded to 0 via '(0 << CLAIMI_PRIO_S)' — guest VMs always see priority=0 in claimi responses regardless of any priority value software wrote to the APLIC_TARGET register. This is a direct violation: the hardware CLAIMI register should return the priority field from the winning interrupt's target configuration.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:449-480  aplic_check_pending
      Returns a boolean after iterating index-order and stopping at the first pending+enabled IRQ matching the target hart. The function is correct for its declared purpose (is any interrupt pending?), but the boolean-only return type structurally guarantees that no priority or identity information can flow through this path to the caller (riscv_sync_interrupts). The invariant that 'all interrupts have equal urgency' is implicit and unenforced — any future use with differentiated priorities would be silently incorrect.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:579-600  riscv_sync_interrupts
      Gateway between VMM APLIC and guest interrupt state: calls aplic_check_pending() to get a boolean and sets hvip.VSEIP as a single bit (hypctx->guest_csrs.hvip |= HVIP_VSEIP). AIA defines hviprio1 and hviprio2 CSRs for per-class interrupt priority fields — these are never populated. The priority data stored in APLIC TARGET registers has zero structural path to influence guest interrupt arbitration. The guest interrupt controller (the emulated VS-level) sees a flat 'external interrupt pending' signal with no priority differentiation.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/intc.c:262-289  intc_intr
      Root interrupt filter dispatches based solely on the scause exception code (software/timer/external IRQ class) with no cross-class priority comparison. In an AIA system, xtopi consolidates priorities across interrupt classes (SSIP, STIP, SEIP) into a single ordered view and selects the highest. The current design treats each interrupt class independently — a lower-priority software interrupt (SSIP) can be serviced before a higher-priority external interrupt (SEIP) if scause arrives with SSIP. The assumption that 'scause arrival order == priority order' is unsafe on AIA-capable hardware.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:306-322  do_trap_supervisor
      Supervisor trap entry reads tf_scause to distinguish interrupt from exception, then dispatches to intr_irq_handler(frame, INTR_ROOT_IRQ). Does not (and structurally cannot) read xtopi CSR to perform AIA-defined priority-based interrupt selection. The scause register reports the architectural cause that trapped, not the highest-priority pending interrupt as ordered by the interrupt controller. On AIA hardware supporting xtopi, the CSR should be the authoritative source for interrupt prioritization — this entry point has no integration for it.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:314-448  aplic_setup_direct_mode
      Initialization sequence sets APLIC domaincfg with IE enabled and direct delivery mode, registers all sources with SM_DETACHED (sources detached from interrupt wires), configures hart_index-to-IDC mapping, and disables IDELIVERY/ITHRESHOLD for all CPUs. Never configures per-source priority values (only later bind_intr writes PRIO=1), never sets non-zero ITHRESHOLD values for priority filtering at the IDC level, and never programs SOURCECFG to SM_EDGE_RSE until setup_intr is called. The baseline configuration leaves the entire priority infrastructure dormant — threshold filtering is off, priorities are flat, and the IDC delivery is disabled until explicitly enabled by bind_intr.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-fast-interrupt/pr-694/

  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:241-262  aplic_handle_idc
      IDC register dispatch treats IDC_TOPI as a no-op (error=0, no value written, no side effects). The TOPI register should report the highest-priority pending interrupt's identity (IRQ number) and priority to the hart, and is the mechanism by which xtopei/xtopi obtains the priority ordering of pending external interrupts. By implementing TOPI as a silent no-op, the VMM guarantees that guest software reading xtopei/xtopi sees nothing — priority-aware interrupt selection is entirely broken in the guest.
  [OK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:264-324  aplic_mmio_access
      Central APLIC MMIO dispatch correctly routes reads/writes to SOURCECFG, TARGET, DOMAINCFG, SETIENUM/CLRIENUM, and IDC registers. The priority fields in TARGET writes are correctly stored via aplic_handle_target (the full 32-bit value including hart_index[31:18] and priority[7:0] is preserved in irq->target). The SOURCECFG values are correctly stored. The MMIO layer itself preserves all AIA-defined fields. The gap is downstream: the stored priority values in irq->target are never consulted by aplic_handle_idc_claimi.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:379-404  do_trap_user
      User-mode trap dispatch mirrors do_trap_supervisor: uses scause-based interrupt identification without xtopi/xtopei integration, then dispatches to intr_irq_handler(frame, INTR_ROOT_IRQ). This is structurally correct for the current non-AIA implementation. AIA interrupt prioritization is primarily an M/S-mode concern; U-mode traps from user-space interrupts would follow the same entry path and the priority gap manifests identically at the supervisor entry point rather than here.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:1-287  (header file — no function)
      No CSR definitions exist for any AIA-related registers: xtopei, xtopi, hstateen (AIA enable bit), hviprio1, hviprio2, hgeie, hgeip are all absent. The header defines SCAUSE codes, SSTATUS bits, MSTATUS bits, HSTATUS bits, MIP/SIP/HVIP bit definitions, SATP fields, and CSR access macros, but none of the AIA-defined CSRs. Without these definitions, no code in the kernel can reference AIA interrupt prioritization registers — making xtopi/xtopei-based arbitration structurally impossible regardless of what any individual function does.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/riscv.h:84-116  (header — struct definitions)
      struct hypcsr carries hvip as a simple uint64_t bitmask without hviprio1/hviprio2 fields. struct hypctx has no fields for interrupt priority state (ipi_pending and interrupts_pending are plain ints, not priority-qualified). struct hyp has no priority maps. The VMM data model has zero storage for the interrupt priority and identity information that xtopei/xtopi would supply. Even if the AIA CSR definitions were added and populated, there would be nowhere to save/restore the priority state across VM entries/exits.


